<title>USA: Hill, W House agree to amend arbitrage rebate rule.</title>
<headline>Hill, W House agree to amend arbitrage rebate rule.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-07-29</dateline>
<text>
<p>U.S. tax legislation agreed to by congressional leaders and the White House would raise from $5 million to $10 million the arbitrage rebate exception for small issuers of public school construction bonds, a bond market lobbyist said Tuesday.</p>
<p>The provision is one of a series of wins for the muni market in the landmark tax legislation.</p>
<p>Another feather in the caps of muni lobbyists was a decision by the White House and House Republicans to drop a plan to repeal the two-percent de minimis rule.</p>
<p>The muni market did not win them all, however. Congressional negotiators rejected a proposal to hike from $10 million to $20 million the ceiling on capital expenditures by firms using small-issue industrial development bonds.</p>
<p>--Vicky Stamas, U.S. Municipals Desk, 202-898-8314</p>
